  "full_response": "# Fibonacci Sequence with Dynamic Programming in Python\n\nHere's a Python function that calculates the Fibonacci sequence using dynamic programming (memoization) to optimize performance:\n\n```python\ndef fibonacci(n, memo={}):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the nth Fibonacci number using dynamic programming (memoization).\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The index in the Fibonacci sequence (0-based)\n        memo (dict): Dictionary to store computed values (used internally)\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The nth Fibonacci number\n    \"\"\"\n    if n in memo:\n        return memo[n]\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    memo[n] = fibonacci(n-1, memo) + fibonacci(n-2, memo)\n    return memo[n]\n\n# Example usage:\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    n = 10\n    print(f\"The {n}th Fibonacci number is: {fibonacci(n)}\")\n\n    # Print first 10 Fibonacci numbers\n    print(\"First 10 Fibonacci numbers:\")\n    for i in range(10):\n        print(fibonacci(i), end=\" \")\n```\n\n### Alternative Implementation (Tabulation/Bottom-up):\n\nHere's another dynamic programming approach using tabulation (iterative with storage):\n\n```python\ndef fibonacci_tabulation(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the nth Fibonacci number using dynamic programming (tabulation).\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The index in the Fibonacci sequence (0-based)\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The nth Fibonacci number\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n\n    # Initialize a list to store Fibonacci numbers\n    fib = [0] * (n + 1)\n    fib[1] = 1\n\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        fib[i] = fib[i-1] + fib[i-2]\n\n    return fib[n]\n```\n\n### Key Features:\n1. **Memoization (Top-down)**: The first implementation stores previously computed values to avoid redundant calculations\n2. **Tabulation (Bottom-up)**: The second implementation builds the solution iteratively from the bottom up\n3. **Efficiency**: Both approaches reduce the time complexity from O(2^n) (naive recursive) to O(n)\n4. **Space Optimization**: The tabulation approach can be further optimized to use O(1) space by only storing the last two values\n\nWould you like me",
